# Badges A (part 1): checked against official scouting.org excerpts.

@owner mb.american-business
@complete complete
@note Sub-items within requirements 1–3 are summarized.
@desc American Business explains how businesses work in a free-enterprise economy: their history, finances, ethics and regulation, and how the five main areas of a business fit together, with a look at business careers.
1 k How business works :: Free enterprise, business history and leaders, the main areas of a business, labor unions and how business affects the economy.
2 k Business finance :: Financial statements, how interest rates, taxes and government spending affect business, raising capital and insurance.
3 k Business decisions :: Profit, sustainable practices, ethics in decisions, and physical versus online businesses.
4 k Workplace laws :: The Department of Labor and major federal laws that protect workers.
5 a Researching a business :: Researching one business across its main areas of operation.
6 a Business careers :: One option: exploring careers in an area of business, or interviewing a business leader.

@owner mb.american-cultures
@complete complete
@desc American Cultures explores the many backgrounds that make up the United States, including your own, through festivals, places, conversations, stories and history, and asks how different groups can understand one another better.
1 a Experiencing other cultures :: Options for learning about three groups first-hand, such as attending an event, visiting a place of worship or institution, or talking with members of a group.
2 d Groups living together :: Imagining each group living on its own and then all three living together.
3 d Customs compared :: Differences and similarities among the groups’ religious and social customs.
4 k Contributions :: Contributions made to the country by people from different backgrounds.
5 a Leading a discussion :: A talk about how different groups have gotten along, and leading a discussion on building understanding.

@owner mb.american-heritage
@complete partial
@note Excerpts confirmed requirements 1, 2, 4 and 5; requirement 3 and any later requirements were not visible and are not outlined yet.
@desc American Heritage connects you with the people, documents, symbols and places that shaped the United States, and with the history of your own family and community.
1 d The Declaration of Independence :: The Declaration of Independence and what it means to you.
2 a People and organizations :: Options for learning about notable Americans, historical organizations, veterans or longtime community members.
4 a Symbols and local history :: Options on national symbols such as the flag, the Pledge, the Great Seal, the motto and the anthem, or on family and local history.
5 a Stories of America :: One option: a film, biography or song about American history, and what it shows.

@owner mb.american-indian-culture
@complete complete
@source https://www.scouting.org/merit-badges/american-indian-culture/ | American Indian Culture merit badge (formerly Indian Lore)
@note Scouting America renamed Indian Lore to American Indian Culture; the official note says the curriculum and requirements were otherwise unchanged by the rename. Confirm whether requirement 5 asks for one or both options.
@desc American Indian Culture (formerly Indian Lore) is a respectful study of the cultural areas, nations and present-day life of American Indian peoples: one tribe or nation in depth, language and place names, leaders, contributions, games, museums and gatherings.
1 k Cultural areas :: The different American Indian cultural areas and what makes each distinct.
2 k Researching a nation :: The way of life, government, beliefs, language, arts and present-day communities of one tribe, group or nation.
3 k Language, places, and leaders :: Words in an American Indian language, the meanings of place names, and notable leaders and their contributions.
4 m Contributions and games :: How American Indians shaped life for newcomers, things others adopted from American Indian cultures, and teaching a traditional game.
5 a Museums and gatherings :: Visiting a museum to study artifacts, or attending a contemporary American Indian gathering and learning its etiquette.

@owner mb.american-labor
@complete partial
@note Excerpts confirmed requirements 1, 3, 4, 5, 7 and 8; requirements 2 and 6 were not visible in current form and are not outlined yet.
@desc American Labor looks at work from the worker’s side: workplace concerns, the history and role of unions, how bargaining works, and how owners, employees and communities balance their goals.
1 a Concerns of workers :: Learning about working people and listing major concerns of American workers, including researching a labor organization.
3 k What unions do :: What labor unions are, what they do and what services they provide.
4 k Bargaining :: Union terms and the difference between adversarial and cooperative bargaining.
5 a Labor history project :: One option, such as a timeline, an exhibit, a film discussion or a biography.
7 d Both sides of an issue :: Arguing a current labor issue from management’s side and then from labor’s.
8 d Goals and compromise :: The different goals of owners, shareholders, customers, employees, unions, communities and officials, and why they compromise.

@owner mb.animal-science
@complete partial
@note Requirement 6 offers options by species (beef, dairy, horse, sheep or goat, hog, or poultry) with their own steps; later requirements were not fully visible.
@desc Animal Science covers the care and management of livestock and poultry: breeds and their uses, disease prevention, nutrition, breeding and production technology, with hands-on work with one type of animal.
@safety Working with large animals requires supervision by an experienced adult.
1 k Breeds :: Breeds of livestock, their uses and origins.
2 k Animal health :: Diseases that affect livestock, their signs and how to prevent them.
3 k Digestion and nutrition :: Differences in digestive systems and how they affect feeding.
4 a A management plan :: A management plan for one type of animal.
5 k Breeding and technology :: Breeding practices and modern production technology.
6 a! Working with one species :: One species option with its own hands-on steps.

@owner mb.animation
@complete complete
@desc Animation introduces how moving images are made: the history and principles of animation, two hands-on animation projects in different techniques, how animation is used in the world, and animation careers. No drawing talent is required.
1 k General knowledge :: What animation is and a brief history of it.
2 k Principles of animation :: Several of the principles that make animation believable.
3 a Animation projects :: Planning, creating and sharing animations in two approved techniques, and what you would improve.
4 a Animation in our world :: Touring an animation studio or business and how animation may be used in the future.
5 a Animation careers :: Animation career options and the training one of them requires.
